0390: cpu: LDUMIN of the LSE emulation stores the minimum

The kernel emulates the LSE atomics the iOS 16 userland uses on these ARMv8.0 cores (sleh.c
sweet_emulate_lse -> machine_routines_asm.s _sweet_lse_{b,h,w,x}). For UMAX (op 6) and UMIN (op 7) the macro
compared the old value with the operand, selected the UMAX result, then compared w1 with 6 to tell the two
apart and selected the UMIN result on the flags of that compare: w1 = 7 is not lower than 6, so LDUMIN stored
the operand whatever the old value was. UMIN now compares the old value with the operand again.

Checked on the phone in EL0 with the macro copied as is (80000 cases per operation and size, against a
reference): before, UMIN mismatched in 40049 of 80000 cases and every other operation (ADD, CLR, EOR, SET,
SMAX, SMIN, UMAX, SWP, CAS) matched; after, 0 mismatches. The emulation in the kernel itself has not been exercised with that test.

--- a/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines_asm.s
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines_asm.s
@@ -1378,10 +1378,15 @@ LEXT(_sweet_lse_\sfx)
 	cmp	\r\()12, \r\()13
 	csel	\r\()11, \r\()10, \r\()2, lt
 	b	5f
+	/*
+	 * sapphire 0390: UMAX (6) and UMIN (7) each select on the flags of their own compare of the old value with the
+	 * operand; UMIN selected on those of "cmp w1, #6" and stored the operand whatever the old value was.
+	 */
 27:	cmp	\r\()10, \r\()2
 	csel	\r\()11, \r\()10, \r\()2, hi
 	cmp	w1, #6
 	b.eq	5f
+	cmp	\r\()10, \r\()2
 	csel	\r\()11, \r\()10, \r\()2, lo
 5:	\stx	w9, \r\()11, [x0]
 	cbnz	w9, 1b
